 "reading": "T1 passes every pre-registered check on XAU/EUR. Paper research only; it says the breakout is in gold's own moves rather than only in the dollar, not that it is an edge to trade. Pooled 2006-04 → 2026-10: +0.067R a trade net (n 3873, month t +3.50; gross +0.099R, t +5.23; +0.035R at 2x costs). H1 2006-16 +0.087R (n 1994, t +3.23; gross t +4.30); H2 2017-26 +0.045R (n 1879, t +1.66; gross t +3.04). 40 of 40 placebo seeds beaten (placebo median −0.009R). Costs take 0.032R a trade. 16 of 21 years positive after costs. For reference the same rule on XAU/USD over the same window (from 2006-11-01): +0.070R net (n 3733, t +3.19; gross t +7.13). On the 3254 days both traded, the two results correlate 0.59 (gross R) and take the same side 86.5 % of the time: on most days the XAU/EUR trade is the XAU/USD trade with the dollar taken out, so gold's own move, not the dollar, sets the side. It is the same effect seen without the dollar leg, not a second, independent one to add beside XAU/USD. The later half alone does not reach t 2 (H2 month t +1.66, net +0.045R), as on XAU/USD over the same years; the pre-registered bar is the pooled t. Before costs the effect is smaller in euros (+0.099R a trade against +0.152R on XAU/USD), so part of the XAU/USD gross may come with the dollar leg; the net figures are close only because the cost here is lower in R. The cost here is a stated 0.016 % of price, not a measured XAU/EUR spread; it charges less in R than the XAU/USD reference's fixed $0.60 in the years gold was cheap. At twice it (0.032 %) the pooled stream is still +0.035R a trade, month t +1.80. Read with the XAU/USD result: the breakout does not depend on the dollar leg. Nothing live changes and no proposal is written."
}
